<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokugawa Yoshikatsu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1824">1824</a>–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tokugawa Yoshikatsu (1824–1883) was lord of the Owari domain from 1849. Punished in the Ansei purge for opposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naosuke">Ii Naosuke</a>, he later commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s first expedition against Chōshū in 1864. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> he sided with the new imperial government, and Owari helped bring other domains over to it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshikatsu.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Yoshikatsu" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gryffindor. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokugawa_Yoshikatsu_(1824%E2%80%931883).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yoshikatsu, whose childhood name was Hidenosuke, was born at the Takasu Domain residence in the Yotsuya district of Edo, the son of Matsudaira Yoshitatsu and Norihime, a daughter of Tokugawa Harutoshi. Tokugawa Nariaki was his maternal uncle, the future shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a> his cousin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-katamori">Matsudaira Katamori</a> his brother. Owari's four previous lords had been adopted from lines close to the shogun and ruled from afar, and low-ranking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the Gold and Iron Party twice pressed for Yoshikatsu, in 1839 and 1845, before he was chosen in 1849.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With weaker ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> than his predecessors, he needed the consent of the senior retainers to govern, and his first measures cut costs. Like Nariaki, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-nariakira">Shimazu Nariakira</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-munenari">Date Munenari</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uwajima">Uwajima</a>, he was troubled by the shogunate's concessions after the Perry Expedition. When the Treaty of Amity and Commerce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was signed in 1858, he joined Nariaki, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-yoshinaga">Matsudaira Yoshinaga</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> and others in an unbidden visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a> to oppose the Tairō Ii Naosuke. He was placed under house arrest and replaced by his younger brother Mochinaga.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naosuke">Ii Naosuke</a>'s assassination in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakuradamon-incident">Sakuradamon Incident</a> of 1860, a general pardon followed in 1862, and Yoshikatsu went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> as an assistant to the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemochi">Tokugawa Iemochi</a>. In 1863 his young son was made the 16th lord as Tokugawa Yoshinori, and Yoshikatsu again wielded power, though the domain split between his supporters and Mochinaga's. He declined a seat on the emperor's council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>, commanded the First Chōshū expedition of September to November 1864 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a> as second-in-command, and refused to take part in the second, which cost the shogunate prestige.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>During his house arrest Yoshikatsu took up Western photography and learned to take and develop pictures himself. Almost 1,000 of his photographs survive, among them the Ninomaru Palace of Nagoya Castle, taken in 1870, Hiroshima Castle and the Owari Domain's suburban residence in Edo. They are valued as a historical record of the last years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and the first years after it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tokugawa Yoshikatsu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1868 <b>Tokugawa Yoshikatsu</b> sided with the new government and purged pro-shogunate retainers.</blockquote><a href="/state/owari-domain">Owari Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1862" aria-label="Edo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tokugawa Yoshikatsu"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4293 4483h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4293 4483h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.68,139.77&amp;year=1862">What was here in 1862? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
